-- DICT-SEMANTICS §8 I1 / §4 `gen`, the ORDER half: "The dictionary parameters a
-- generalized binding abstracts -- their COUNT, ORDER, and predicates -- are part
-- of that binding's elaborated type", and §4 `gen`: producers abstract, consumers
-- apply, "and the two must agree on ORDER AND ARITY of the dictionary
-- parameters".
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): `pair2 : (Aa p, Bb q) => p -> q -> Int` abstracts
-- TWO dict params, in the declared order (`Aa` then `Bb`), and every call site
-- applies them in that same order. At `pair2 3 5`, `av 3` = 3 and `bv 5` = 500,
-- so the result is 503.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all ACCEPT and print 503. CONFORMANT. The emitted
-- `define` for `pair2` takes FOUR parameters (2 dicts + 2 values), asserted
-- structurally in the gate's IR section.
--
-- The two impls are deliberately asymmetric (`av n = n`, `bv n = n * 100`) and
-- both live at `Int`, so the ONLY thing distinguishing the two dicts is their
-- POSITION. A producer/consumer disagreement on order -- passing the `Bb` dict
-- where `Aa` is expected -- swaps the two methods and yields 300 + 5 = 305, or
-- crashes; it cannot coincidentally still print 503. Had the two interfaces been
-- at different types, unification would have masked the swap.
--
-- Added after an adversarial review (F2) observed that every other ACCEPTED
-- fixture in this corpus abstracts AT MOST ONE dict, and the only two-predicate
-- signature in the corpus (s9-vector-valued-entailment-rejected) is a REJECT row
-- that never reaches elaboration -- so dict-param ORDER was never exercised at
-- all, while §8 I1's row read as covered.
interface Aa a where
  av : a -> Int

interface Bb b where
  bv : b -> Int

impl Aa Int where
  av n = n

impl Bb Int where
  bv n = n * 100

pair2 : (Aa p, Bb q) => p -> q -> Int
pair2 x y = av x + bv y

main = println (pair2 (3 : Int) (5 : Int))
